The Annandale Farm HCA enjoys very high associative historic heritage values due to George Johnston's Annandale Farm, one of the most important locations in the early days of the Colony and the centre of a vast pastoral empire.

There is high archaeological potential relating to deposits from Annandale Farm's layers of development.

The range of development types includes high quality railway mansions and villas to modest single-fronted row houses.

The local shopping centre is high quality and substantially intact.

Speculative development patterns are evident in built forms.

The HCA includes groups of high quality and substantially intact late 19th century and early 20th century houses, terraces and semi-detached cottages.

Cohesive streetscapes continue to demonstrate original development patterns.

The strong and regular streetscape rhythm is a result of regular subdivision patterns and prevalence of speculative development practices.

